\title{Adjacency--Degree Matrix Moments as Degree Statistics\\and Star Homomorphism Counts}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 31, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Let $G$ be a finite simple undirected graph with adjacency matrix $A$, diagonal degree matrix $D$, and all-ones vector $\one$. We establish
\[
 \one^TADA\one=\sum_{v\in V(G)}d(v)^3=\homc(K_{1,3},G).
\]
Thus the scalar adjacency--degree word $ADA$ is simultaneously the third raw moment of the degree sequence and the number of graph homomorphisms from the three-leaf star. We first prove a more general result for arbitrary finite symmetric real matrices: if $r$ is the row-sum vector and $D_r$ is its diagonal matrix, then $\one^TAD_rA\one=\sum_i r_i^3$. We then identify row sums of a graph adjacency matrix with vertex degrees and give a direct interpretation in terms of ordered triples of neighbors. The connector supplies equivalent computational algorithms, clarifies sensitivity to high-degree vertices, and gives a base case for a broader dictionary between adjacency--degree words and degree-decorated tree homomorphisms. We discuss complexity, limitations, weighted extensions, higher star moments, and applications to network analysis.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
A finite graph admits several complementary descriptions. Combinatorially, it is a set of vertices joined by edges. Algebraically, it is represented by an adjacency matrix. Statistically, it has a degree distribution. Homomorphism theory counts structure-preserving maps from small patterns into the graph. This paper gives an exact bridge among these descriptions.

Let $A$ be the adjacency matrix of a finite simple undirected graph, $D$ its diagonal degree matrix, and $\one$ the all-ones vector. We study the scalar $\one^TADA\one$. Although this appears to describe two propagation steps through the graph, it collapses to
\[
 \one^TADA\one=\sum_v d(v)^3.
\]
The right side is the third raw power sum of the degree multiset and strongly emphasizes hubs. It also counts homomorphisms from the three-leaf star $K_{1,3}$. If its center maps to $v$, each of its three labeled leaves independently maps to any neighbor of $v$, giving $d(v)^3$ choices.

We begin with a matrix theorem independent of graphs. This separates the roles of the assumptions: symmetry converts column sums into row sums, adjacency matrices convert row sums into degrees, and the Cartesian-product rule turns degree cubes into star homomorphisms. The identity is also a prototype for a larger program in which noncommutative words in $A$ and $D$ encode degree-decorated pattern counts.

\section{Definitions and notation}

\begin{definition}[Finite simple graph]
A finite simple undirected graph is a pair $G=(V,E)$, where $V$ is finite and $E$ consists of two-element subsets of $V$. Vertices $u,v$ are adjacent, written $u\sim v$, when $\{u,v\}\in E$. The neighborhood and degree of $v$ are
\[
 N(v)=\{u\in V:u\sim v\},\qquad d(v)=|N(v)|.
\]
\end{definition}

After ordering the $n=|V|$ vertices, the adjacency matrix $A\in\{0,1\}^{n\times n}$ is defined by $A_{uv}=1$ exactly when $u\sim v$. Undirectedness gives $A^T=A$. The degree matrix is
\[
 D=\diag(d(v):v\in V),
\]
and $\one\in\R^n$ has every coordinate equal to one.

\begin{definition}[Row sums and matrix moment]
For a real matrix $A=(A_{ij})$ indexed by a finite set, its row-sum vector is
\[
 r_i=\sum_j A_{ij},
\]
so $r=A\one$. Write $D_r=\diag(r)$. For symmetric $A$, define the adjacency--row-sum moment by
\[
 \mu_{ADA}(A)=\one^TAD_rA\one.
\]
For a graph adjacency matrix this is the adjacency--degree moment $\mu_{ADA}(G)=\one^TADA\one$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Degree power sum]
For $q\geq 0$, the $q$th raw degree power sum is
\[
 p_q(G)=\sum_{v\in V}d(v)^q.
\]
If $X$ is a uniformly random vertex, then $\mathbb E[d(X)^q]=p_q(G)/|V|$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Graph homomorphism]
For finite simple graphs $H$ and $G$, a graph homomorphism is a map $f:V(H)\to V(G)$ such that every edge $xy$ of $H$ maps to an edge $f(x)f(y)$ of $G$. Let $\homc(H,G)$ be the number of such maps. The map need not be injective.
\end{definition}

The star $K_{1,3}$ has a center $c$, three labeled leaves $\ell_1,\ell_2,\ell_3$, and one edge from $c$ to each leaf.

\section{The symmetric matrix identity}

\begin{theorem}[Symmetric Row-Sum Moment Theorem]
Let $A\in\R^{V\times V}$ be symmetric on a finite index set, let $r=A\one$, and let $D_r=\diag(r)$. Then
\[
 \one^TAD_rA\one=\sum_{i\in V}r_i^3.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By definition, $A\one=r$. Symmetry gives
\[
 \one^TA=(A^T\one)^T=(A\one)^T=r^T.
\]
Therefore $\one^TAD_rA\one=r^TD_rr$. Since $D_r$ is diagonal, the contribution of coordinate $i$ is $r_i\,r_i\,r_i=r_i^3$. Summing proves the claim.
\end{proof}

An index expansion exposes the same mechanism:
\[
 \one^TAD_rA\one
 =\sum_{i,j}A_{ij}r_j^2
 =\sum_j\left(\sum_iA_{ij}\right)r_j^2.
\]
For a symmetric matrix, the $j$th column sum is the $j$th row sum $r_j$.

\begin{remark}[What happens without symmetry]
If $c_j=\sum_iA_{ij}$ is the column-sum vector, then in general
\[
 \one^TAD_rA\one=\sum_jc_jr_j^2.
\]
Equality with $\sum_jr_j^3$ holds whenever $c=r$; symmetry is a natural sufficient condition.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}[Weighted networks]
The theorem allows arbitrary real entries. In a symmetric weighted network, $r_i$ is vertex strength and the moment is the sum of cubes of strengths. Negative weights preserve the algebraic identity, although the literal counting interpretation becomes a signed weighted sum.
\end{remark}

\section{Specialization to graphs}

\begin{lemma}[Adjacency Row-Sum Lemma]
For every vertex $v$ of a finite simple undirected graph,
\[
 (A\one)_v=\sum_uA_{vu}=d(v).
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The summand $A_{vu}$ is one precisely for $u\in N(v)$ and zero otherwise, so the sum counts $N(v)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Adjacency--Degree Moment Theorem]
For every finite simple undirected graph $G$,
\[
 \mu_{ADA}(G)=\one^TADA\one=\sum_{v\in V(G)}d(v)^3=p_3(G).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The adjacency matrix is symmetric, its row-sum vector is the degree vector by the preceding lemma, and its row-sum diagonal matrix is $D$. Apply the Symmetric Row-Sum Moment Theorem.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Relabeling invariance]
The scalar $\one^TADA\one$ is invariant under graph isomorphism and vertex relabeling.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
A relabeling has a permutation matrix $P$, with $A'=PAP^T$, $D'=PDP^T$, and $P\one=\one$. Substitution yields $\one^TA'D'A'\one=\one^TADA\one$. Equivalently, the degree multiset is invariant.
\end{proof}

\section{Star homomorphisms}

\begin{lemma}[Ordered Neighbor-Triples]
For every vertex $v$,
\[
 d(v)^3=|N(v)\times N(v)\times N(v)|.
\]
Consequently,
\[
 \sum_vd(v)^3=|\{(v,x,y,z):x,y,z\in N(v)\}|.
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Cardinality is multiplicative over finite Cartesian products. Retaining the center $v$ makes the families for distinct centers disjoint.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Star Homomorphism Lemma]
For every finite simple graph $G$,
\[
 \homc(K_{1,3},G)=\sum_{v\in V(G)}d(v)^3.
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Partition the homomorphisms according to the image $v$ of the center. Each labeled leaf can independently map to any element of $N(v)$, giving $d(v)^3$ maps for that center. Sum over $v$.
\end{proof}

The leaves are distinct in the source but may share images. Thus this count is neither $\binom{d(v)}3$ nor the number of embedded stars.

\begin{theorem}[Matrix--Statistics--Homomorphism Correspondence]
For every finite simple undirected graph $G$,
\[
 \boxed{\one^TADA\one=\sum_{v\in V(G)}d(v)^3=\homc(K_{1,3},G)}.
\]
Equivalently, this scalar is the number of ordered quadruples $(v,x,y,z)$ for which $x,y,z$ are all adjacent to $v$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Combine the Adjacency--Degree Moment Theorem with the Star Homomorphism Lemma.
\end{proof}

\section{Algorithms and complexity}
Let $n=|V|$ and $m=|E|$. The correspondence gives three algorithms.

\paragraph{Degree-power method.}
Compute each degree and sum its cube. With adjacency lists already present, reading lengths and summing takes $O(n)$ time; building degrees from an edge list and summing takes $O(n+m)$ time. This is normally the fastest method for sparse graphs.

\paragraph{Matrix-word method.}
Compute successively $x=A\one$, $y=Dx$, $z=Ay$, and $S=\one^Tz$. Dense matrix-vector multiplication costs $O(n^2)$, while sparse multiplication costs $O(n+m)$. Forming full dense products is unnecessary and would cost $O(n^3)$ naively.

\paragraph{Explicit homomorphism enumeration.}
For each center $v$, loop independently over $x,y,z\in N(v)$. This takes
\[
 O\!\left(\sum_vd(v)^3\right)
\]
time, proportional to the output count. It is useful when the maps themselves are needed.

Computing by multiple routes gives a consistency check. Disagreement can reveal accidental asymmetry, incorrect degree extraction, wrong matrix order, or an enumeration that mistakenly requires distinct leaves.

\section{Examples}
For an empty graph every degree is zero, so the moment and homomorphism count vanish. For $K_2$, both degrees are one and the value is $2$.

For the path $P_n$ with $n\geq2$, two endpoints have degree one and $n-2$ internal vertices have degree two. Hence
\[
 \mu_{ADA}(P_n)=2+8(n-2)=8n-14.
\]
Every vertex of a cycle $C_n$ has degree two, so $\mu_{ADA}(C_n)=8n$.

For the star $K_{1,m}$, the center has degree $m$ and the leaves degree one, giving
\[
 \mu_{ADA}(K_{1,m})=m^3+m.
\]
The term $m^3$ arises when the source center maps to the target center; the additional $m$ maps arise when it maps to a target leaf. For the complete graph $K_n$, every degree is $n-1$, so the value is $n(n-1)^3$.

The invariant is not complete. The cycle $C_6$ and the disjoint union $C_3\sqcup C_3$ both have six degree-two vertices, hence both yield $48$, but only the first is connected.

\section{Statistical interpretation}
If $X$ is a uniformly random vertex, then
\[
 \frac1n\one^TADA\one=\mathbb E[d(X)^3].
\]
The convexity of $x\mapsto x^3$ on nonnegative reals makes the observable sensitive to hubs. If $G$ has $m$ edges, Jensen's inequality gives
\[
 \frac1n\sum_vd(v)^3\geq\left(\frac{2m}{n}\right)^3,
 \qquad
 \one^TADA\one\geq\frac{(2m)^3}{n^2}.
\]
Equality in the real-valued inequality occurs when all degrees are equal. Thus, when regularity is feasible, regular graphs minimize the cubic power sum at fixed average degree. The moment nevertheless depends only on the degree multiset and does not reveal how degrees are arranged.

\section{Prospective higher star moments}
The established identity suggests a uniform extension based on the words $AD^kA$. The proposed program is to prove, for every nonnegative integer $k$, that $\one^TAD^kA\one$ is the degree power sum $\sum_vd(v)^{k+2}$ and the homomorphism count of the star having $k+2$ leaves. The theorem proved here supplies the first degree-decorated case, $k=1$.

If this family is developed uniformly, an $n$-vertex graph's first $n$ star moments can be combined with Newton's identities. Those identities recover the elementary symmetric polynomials from the power sums, thereby recovering $\prod_{v\in V}(t-d(v))$ and hence the degree multiset. This would reconstruct degree data, not generally the full graph.

\section{Adjacency--degree words}
The matrices $A$ and $D$ need not commute. In a word such as $AD^2AD$, every $A$ aggregates across neighbors while every $D^q$ weights the current coordinate at $v$ by $d(v)^q$. Expanding a scalar $\one^Tw(A,D)\one$ suggests a sum over walks with degree powers decorating visited vertices.

This motivates a prospective dictionary with degree-decorated caterpillars. Adjacency factors should trace a path-like spine, while degree factors should count independent choices of attached leaves. The three-leaf star theorem is the established base case. Building this dictionary would permit broader algebraic observables to be interpreted as pattern counts and combinatorial configurations to be aggregated through matrix operations.

\section{Applications}
The normalized moment is a hub-sensitive summary for communication, biological, transportation, and social networks. The theorem also gives a closed formula for a homomorphism motif count, avoiding explicit enumeration when only the count is needed.

For symmetric weighted interaction systems, the matrix theorem computes the cubic moment of vertex strengths. With nonnegative weights it is a weighted three-star partition sum. The three equivalent computations also provide a data-validation invariant for pipelines that store edges, matrices, and degree summaries separately.

Finally, collections of adjacency--degree moments can serve as interpretable isomorphism-invariant features. The present coordinate is not a complete invariant, but unlike an opaque numerical feature its meaning is exact in algebraic, statistical, and combinatorial terms.

\section{Limitations}
For directed matrices, row sums and column sums differ; if the middle diagonal contains out-degrees, the analogous scalar is a mixed sum of in-degree times squared out-degree. Homomorphism counts also differ from injective subgraph counts: distinct source leaves may coincide in the target. Requiring distinct leaf images replaces $d(v)^3$ by the falling factorial $d(v)(d(v)-1)(d(v)-2)$. Negative matrix weights preserve the algebra but turn counts into signed weighted sums. Most importantly, this moment and even the full family of star moments cannot distinguish graphs sharing a degree multiset.

\section{Future directions}
Natural next steps include:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
\item developing arbitrary star moments uniformly and implementing degree-multiset reconstruction with Newton identities;
\item assigning a degree-decorated caterpillar to each word in $A$ and $D$ and proving the corresponding counting identity;
\item studying the vector space generated from $\one$ by adjacency--degree words and its invariance under automorphisms;
\item determining when this cyclic space equals the span of automorphism-orbit indicators, especially for forests;
\item investigating whether all scalar adjacency--degree word moments characterize finite trees;
\item comparing generated spaces with equitable partitions and one-dimensional color refinement; and
\item analyzing small switching constructions that preserve selected moments while changing graph isomorphism type.
\end{enumerate}
The first directions extend the explicit connector proved here; the later questions require deeper graph-automorphism and finite-dimensional algebra.

\section{Conclusion}
For every finite simple undirected graph,
\[
 \one^TADA\one=\sum_vd(v)^3=\homc(K_{1,3},G).
\]
The proof rests on three transparent observations: adjacency sends the all-ones vector to row sums, graph row sums are degrees, and a degree cube counts ordered choices of three neighbors. Symmetry aligns the row and column contributions. The resulting bridge supplies equivalent algorithms, a hub-sensitive statistic, a weighted extension, and a base case for interpreting richer matrix words as decorated graph homomorphism counts.

\end{document}

