# Explore the Adjacency–Degree Moment

A single network observable connects matrix algebra, degree statistics, and graph-pattern counting:

$$\mathbf 1^TADA\mathbf 1=\sum_v d(v)^3=\operatorname{hom}(K_{1,3},G).$$

Start with the **Degree Moment Studio**. Enter any degree list and watch cubic weighting amplify hubs.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

The same calculation can be performed directly from an edge list.

{{demo:0}}

## Three equivalent computational routes

The sparse degree-power algorithm is generally the most efficient route when the graph is represented by adjacency lists.

{{algorithm:0}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal why the matrix formula collapses</summary>

Let $d=A\mathbf 1$. Since an undirected graph has $A^T=A$, we also have $\mathbf 1^TA=d^T$. Therefore

$$\mathbf 1^TADA\mathbf 1=d^TDd=\sum_vd(v)^3.$$

The diagonal matrix $D$ supplies one degree factor, while the vectors on its two sides supply the other two.
</details>

{{algorithm:1}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal the homomorphism interpretation</summary>

Choose the image $v$ of the center of $K_{1,3}$. Each of three labeled leaves independently chooses one of $d(v)$ neighbors. Repetition is allowed, giving $d(v)^3$ maps for this center and $\sum_vd(v)^3$ in total.
</details>

{{demo:1}}

## Compare graph families

The next explorer contrasts paths, cycles, and stars on the same number of vertices. Paths and cycles have bounded degree, whereas a star’s hub grows with network size.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{visualization:0}}

{{visualization:1}}

## Beyond the cubic moment

A natural next step is to develop the proposed family connecting the words $AD^kA$, higher degree power sums, and stars with more leaves. The numerical widget below explores the degree-power and star-count side of that prospective extension.

Explore how higher powers increasingly focus on high-degree vertices.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{visualization:2}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal degree-multiset reconstruction</summary>

For an $n$-vertex graph, the first $n$ degree power sums determine the elementary symmetric polynomials through [Newton's identities](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newton%27s_identities). These are the coefficients of $\prod_v(t-d(v))$, whose roots recover the degree multiset.

{{algorithm:2}}
</details>

## What the observable cannot see

The cycle $C_6$ and two disjoint triangles are both $2$-regular on six vertices. Both have moment $48$, although one is connected and the other is not. Degree moments are interpretable invariants, not complete graph fingerprints. For background on the surrounding language, see [graph homomorphisms](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Graph_homomorphism), [adjacency matrices](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adjacency_matrix), and [degree distributions](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Degree_distribution).

